fund-csharp/22-httpclient-timeouts-retri.explanation:
A linked source carries both reasons to stop, and the exception filter reads the caller's token to tell them apart:

```csharp
using var attempt = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(caller);
attempt.CancelAfter(timeout);
try { return new CallResult(200, await call(attempt.Token)); }
catch (OperationCanceledException) when (caller.IsCancellationRequested) { throw; }
catch (OperationCanceledException) { return new CallResult(504, null); }
catch (HttpRequestException) { return new CallResult(502, null); }
```

The tests catch three plausible wrong versions. A single `catch (OperationCanceledException)` that always answers 504 hands back a result where both caller tests expect the exception. Passing `caller` straight to the call means the timeout never cancels anything, so the two 504 tests run into their 2-second guard and fail. A `new CancellationTokenSource(timeout)` that is not linked ignores a caller who leaves: "a caller that leaves mid-call gets the cancellation, not a 504" hits its guard, and the boundary test gets a 200 instead of the cancellation.

